cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  corpus-access-bench: open-book versus closed-book performance of
  language models across a capability range
message: If you use this benchmark or its protocol, please cite it.
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Ha-Duong
    given-names: Minh
    orcid: https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9988-2100
    affiliation: CNRS, CIRED
repository-code: https://github.com/MinhHaDuong/corpus-access-bench
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22044030
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-08-21
license: CC-BY-4.0
keywords:
  - large language models
  - retrieval
  - evaluation methodology
  - blind grading
  - pre-registration
  - local inference
  - history of economic thought
abstract: >-
  Sixty doctoral-level questions in the history of economic thought, sat by four
  language models under five ways of reaching the same specialist corpus: cold
  with no tools, the open web, the corpus as PDFs with text search, a hand-built
  index of it, and the corpus cut into one file per entry. Every copy graded
  blind by three judges from a different model family, against decision rules
  frozen before each run and a salted copy carrying sealed defects that
  calibrates the panel. Three results. Corpus access moves a local 27B model by
  20.3 marks out of 60 and the strongest frontier model by 1.7, against an
  instrument that resolves about 2. The fully equipped 27B still scores 44.9
  where a frontier model with no tools at all scores 56.0. And the hand-built
  index never beat plain text search in any model, while the file-per-entry
  directory was the only access layer to move anything by double digits, in
  opposite directions depending on the model. The forty discriminating questions
  are sealed and recorded by hash; the twenty-question control stratum is
  published whole, with every arm's answers and every judge's per-question line.
